WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.636
-Our next guest is an
Emmy-winning actor you know from

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.137
"Veep" and
"Arrested Development."

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:07.139
He stars in "Hocus Pocus 2,"
which is streaming now

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:08.174
on Disney+, as well as

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.042
"The Mysterious
Benedict Society."

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:13.179
The second season premiers
October 26th on Disney+.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.547
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:17.016
-You think I'll be elated to
hear you continue to claim

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:18.818
you ended the emergency?

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:22.521
A global crisis you're
wholly responsible for creating?

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:26.225
-Drop this ridiculous facade and
offer the truth -- now.

00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:28.494
-No, it's not a facade.
-Oh!

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:33.165
-I believe the instigator of
a moment of change

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:35.601
can come from
an unexpected direction.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:39.072
-Please welcome back to the show
Tony Hale, everybody.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.174
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.755
Welcome back.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.556
I'm so happy to have you
here in person.

00:00:57.556 --> 00:00:58.625
-It's so nice to see everybody.

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.059
Your Trump impression
is very good.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:02.027
-Thank you, you know?
-Very good.

00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:04.463
-It was almost worth it for him
to be president, it's so good.

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:06.466
I sometimes --
I mean person-- No.

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:08.133
-Okay.

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:09.702
-So, I want to talk about
"The Benedict Society,"

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.638
but also -- So, "Hocus Pocus" --
you're in the sequel.

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:13.973
-Yes.
-A long-time-coming sequel.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.708
-Mm-hmm.
-This is a Halloween classic.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.811
Are you someone who likes
a good Halloween-themed film?

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:19.879
-No.
-Okay.

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:22.648
I think --
I would say "Hocus Pocus"

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:23.983
is kind of a level I like.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.051
-Okay. So that's about
as scary as you want to go.

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:31.124
-Yeah. But I genuinely need
someone to explain to me --

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.626
There is so much fear
in the world.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:34.960
I experience so much fear.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.030
Why would I want to sit in
a room with other people

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.365
and watch people be murdered?

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:40.433
-Yeah.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.069
-It doesn't --
I genuinely don't get it.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.372
I don't get why people pay money
to go through haunted houses.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.941
I'm like -- If I'm home alone,

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.842
I'm thinking someone's
going to jump out at me.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:53.046
-So that's your haunted house
is just being home alone.

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:55.147
-That's a Tuesday.

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:58.450
But I really -- I genuinely --
Here's the thing.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.120
People can watch
those scary movies

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.755
and walk away and be like,
"Well, it was fun."

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.458
I'm like, "Someone's after me,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.360
and I need to protect myself
and my family."

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.096
-So, I mean, at least those are,

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:12.431
you know,
by and large, fictional.

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:13.532
What do you think about, like,

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:14.933
true-crime
documentaries and stuff?

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:16.169
-Dude, come on.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:20.006
I genuinely --
Like the Dahmer show?

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.073
-Yeah.

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:23.275
-No, I'm not going to
watch that.

00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:26.846
It's so terrifying,
and that's --

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.048
Like, watch CNN
and be just as freaked out.

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:32.051
-Right.
-Like, why are we doing this?

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:34.154
I'll watch, like,
YouTube videos of, like,

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:35.621
puppies coming home
to their owners

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:37.856
and stuff like that, you know?

00:02:37.856 --> 00:02:39.225
-Usually, it's the owners
coming home to puppies,

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.393
but you watch --

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:43.630
-I just watch puppies
come home to owners.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:47.166
-I mean, I didn't even
know those existed.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.336
And then do the owners,
like, freak out?

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.638
-Oh!

00:02:55.908 --> 00:02:57.810
-Those are...
-Woof, woof!

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.749
-From serving overseas.
The dogs have served overseas.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.053
-There's a lot of
YouTubes like that.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:09.155
-So, this is -- First of all,

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.456
the clip of
"The Benedict Society."

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:13.025
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Very cool. You play two parts.

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:14.893
You play twins --
one good, one evil.

00:03:14.893 --> 00:03:17.563
You also played two parts
in the new "Hocus Pocus" film.

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:19.431
And I think that
speaks to you as an a actor that

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:23.302
multiple directors have said,
"You should do both."

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:24.837
-Yeah.
Well, it's in my contract.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:28.307
-Oh, so you only will now work
if you get to do two parts.

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:30.075
-Yeah. Scorsese will call,
and I'll be like,

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:32.512
"Does he have a twin?"

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:36.148
-So, have you said "no"
to Scorsese?

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:38.184
-Many times over.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:40.753
Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:43.489
All the parts that went to
DiCaprio were originally you,

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:44.590
but he was like, "There's --"

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.859
-I was like,
"He doesn't have a twin."

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.096
-I like that you won't play
two different parts.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:52.098
They have to be a twin, too.

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.533
-No, twin.
-It's like, "I got to parts.

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.001
I can play two parts.
I can play this guy, that guy."

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:56.368
You're like --
-Nope, twin.

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.805
Identical twin.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:03.676
-These -- You know, being a
show, a wonderful kid's show,

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.145
do kids recognize you
on the street now?

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:08.647
-For "Benedict Society"?
Yeah, with a beard, they do.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:10.482
Without the beard, they don't.

00:04:10.482 --> 00:04:11.817
There's sometimes, though --
It's really sweet.

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:14.687
Like, if somebody --
When I did Forky,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:15.922
someone will come up
and be like,

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.856
"Oh, he was the voice of Forky."

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:19.558
And the kid's like,
"That's not Forky."

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:21.160
And, so, it's really --
Oh, yeah, there he is.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.394
-We've got a picture of Forky.

00:04:22.394 --> 00:04:25.264
And, so, it should be --
I mean, I don't blame kids

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:27.667
for not thinking
that's you, right?

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.836
-Yeah. That's along
the realms of dogs

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:31.770
coming home to their owners.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.173
But it's really fun
because I can do --

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:36.642
Like, I'll send
a little voice memo to them,

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:37.776
and their parents will play it.

00:04:37.776 --> 00:04:38.945
And they'll show a video.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.445
They'll play the video
of them hearing it,

00:04:40.445 --> 00:04:41.581
and that's really fun.

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:43.549
'Cause they think Forky is real.

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.551
'Cause he is.

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:48.287
-You don't give it up
in the voice memo.

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:51.023
You don't, as Forky,
explain that "I'm always --

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:53.158
I've never been
real this whole time."

00:04:53.158 --> 00:04:56.296
-And guess what else
is not real. No, no, no.

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.330
-But, you know,
guess what is real.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.299
Jeffrey Dahmer.
And you can...

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:01.567
-Thank you.
-That was real.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.402
-By the way, thank you.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:07.706
So, why are we -- Why is this,
like, the top-rated show?

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:08.741
That's upsetting.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.009
-It is very upsetting.
-Come on.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:13.045
-You've been in a lot of shows

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:15.748
that I feel like
are quoted all the time.

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.583
You show up TikTok all the time.

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:18.817
"Veep" and
"Arrested Development."

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:20.319
Which I think speaks to
both your performance

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.187
and how incredibly
tightly written

00:05:22.187 --> 00:05:25.357
those shows were
that they sort of endure.

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:27.126
Is that --
Do people come up to you

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.995
and quote lines
back to you all the time?

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:31.797
-Yeah, and there's a lot of
jokes I miss.

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:34.333
A lot, like,
-So, that you were part of.

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:35.434
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:39.238
-Like, there's this joke
in "Arrested"

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:41.140
where my hand
is eaten off by a seal.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.441
I don't know
if you guys remember.

00:05:42.441 --> 00:05:45.077
And there was a great scene
in there where, like,

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:46.912
the doctor comes up,
and they say, "How's Buster?"

00:05:46.912 --> 00:05:48.313
'Cause my character is Buster.

00:05:48.313 --> 00:05:51.216
And they said, "He's okay,
but he lost his left arm."

00:05:51.216 --> 00:05:52.385
It's kind of funny.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:56.422
But then I'm doing
this podcast once,

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:58.024
and they said, "No, no, no,
that's not the joke.

00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:00.392
The joke is, he said,
'He's all right,'

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:03.695
meaning he only has
a right hand."

00:06:03.695 --> 00:06:07.599
Guys, that was 15 years
after I shot the show

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:09.468
that I got that joke.

00:06:09.468 --> 00:06:10.903
That's sad.

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:14.807
Oh, and, recently,
I went to this great restaurant

00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:17.543
in Raleigh, North Carolina,
called Union Special.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:18.978
And they gave me this T-shirt,
and it said,

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:20.212
"Waking and baking."

00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:23.315
And I was like,
"Oh, you know, I love bread."

00:06:23.315 --> 00:06:25.684
So, I had -- So, like,

00:06:25.684 --> 00:06:27.019
I wore this shirt
for like two years.

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.722
I wore this shirt to drop my
daughter off to school.

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:33.592
I wore this shirt to church.

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:35.628
And I would say
probably like two months ago,

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:36.729
someone was like,
"Oh, you did a little..."

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:37.830
[ Clicks tongue ]

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.632
And I was like, "What?"

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:43.635
I had no idea
it was about the weed.

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:45.370
-By the way,
even if it was about baking,

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.973
it does seem pretty
underdressed for church.

00:06:47.973 --> 00:06:50.242
-Not my church.
-Okay.

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:52.544
-You got the laid-back kind?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:52.544 --> 00:06:54.279
-Well, this makes sense that
someone that would wear

00:06:54.279 --> 00:06:57.116
a T-shirt to church --
you did get your start here

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:58.918
in New York City
in the fashion industry.

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.387
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:02.622
Okay.

00:07:02.622 --> 00:07:05.090
I temped.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.160
One of my first temp jobs
was at "Vogue."

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.195
-Okay. So, you were just a temp,

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:11.296
and you got assigned to
"Vogue" one day.

00:07:11.296 --> 00:07:14.032
-Yeah. I was at this
temp agency and I was --

00:07:14.032 --> 00:07:16.235
Because I was auditioning
for commercials,

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:17.503
and you couldn't do a set gig.

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:18.804
You have to do it day-to-day.
-Right. Of course.

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:20.273
-And so, every day,
they'd send me somewhere new,

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.908
and they sent me to "Vogue."

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:24.276
And it was just like --
I was just like --

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:27.279
I looked like a bad Gap ad
in the middle of all --

00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:29.781
Just khakis and,
like, surrounded by --

00:07:29.781 --> 00:07:30.916
I was like a stain
in the fashion world.

00:07:30.916 --> 00:07:32.752
-Did you clock it
immediately when you --

00:07:32.752 --> 00:07:34.786
Did you expect it to be
that jarring

00:07:34.786 --> 00:07:36.221
or was it when you walked in
that you realized,

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:37.423
"Oh, I'm not dressed --"

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:40.592
-Oh, It was immediate.
But I love those jobs.

00:07:40.592 --> 00:07:43.262
For those of you
who have temped, back then,

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:44.896
we didn't have the cellphones
and all that stuff.

00:07:44.896 --> 00:07:46.332
And I would make just

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:47.967
long-distance phone calls
all day.

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:49.468
I would just sit at the desk,
and, you know,

00:07:49.468 --> 00:07:51.236
there's Anna Wintour,
and I was just like, "Hey, Mom."

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:54.173
Like, I was just making
long-distance phone calls.

00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:56.375
So Wintour paid for my
long-distance phone calls.

00:07:56.375 --> 00:07:57.509
-Oh, that's really nice.

00:07:57.509 --> 00:07:59.011
And then, but that
was the end of it, right?

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:01.446
You had no communication with
"Vogue" after that.

00:08:01.446 --> 00:08:04.350
-Well, they've tried to
contact me many times.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.550
-They wanted to put you
on the cover,

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:06.985
but you were like, "As a twin?"

00:08:06.985 --> 00:08:09.355
-Yeah. No.

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:11.991
Cut to a foldout.

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:13.959
-That was a very weird
September issue

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:17.763
when it was you
and then you folded it out.

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:20.333
-The month that "Vogue"
just goes to crap.

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:22.467
-You mentioned that kids only
recognize you with a beard.

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:25.037
Is the beard now something
you only have for the part

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:28.106
or are you bearded
in regular life now?

00:08:28.106 --> 00:08:29.942
-I like the beard.
-The beard's really good.

00:08:29.942 --> 00:08:31.410
-It's, like, a natural
kind of fat contour.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.345
-Uh-huh.
-I kind of like it.

00:08:33.345 --> 00:08:35.214
-I'm curious, though.
I know you have a daughter.

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:36.615
Does she like the beard?

00:08:36.615 --> 00:08:39.885
Because when my boys -- Even
when I grow it out a little bit,

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:43.222
they don't care for it at all.

00:08:43.222 --> 00:08:45.224
-Yeah, I think -- Yeah.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:50.630
Mine's 16 now, and she's --
She'll be 17 in February.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:54.900
-Wow. What piece of your work
does she like -- None of it.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.636
-None of it.
-Ever. At any point?

00:08:56.636 --> 00:08:58.203
I mean, 'cause I guess,
like, Forky,

00:08:58.203 --> 00:09:01.540
she's too old for Forky
when it happens, probably, or...

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.941
-Yeah, she was
too old for Forky,

00:09:02.941 --> 00:09:05.377
but even when she was little,
we didn't watch

00:09:05.377 --> 00:09:06.545
"Arrested Development."

00:09:06.545 --> 00:09:07.679
I mean --

00:09:07.679 --> 00:09:09.882
-Yeah, that's not
a great little --

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:11.984
By the way, I love that you're
like, "Dahmer's too rough,"

00:09:11.984 --> 00:09:13.619
and then you told us,
"There's this really funny scene

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:14.820
where a seal eats my arm."

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.255
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:18.524
And then the seal eats it.

00:09:18.524 --> 00:09:21.526
But there is this one scene
on "Arrested" where --

00:09:21.526 --> 00:09:23.428
just talk about a picture of
co-dependency --

00:09:23.428 --> 00:09:27.099
where my mother,
played by Jessica Walter,

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:29.468
the late Jessica Walter,
who was wonderful.

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:31.770
She was on house arrest
and she couldn't smoke.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:37.209
And Buster had to inhale
the smoke out of her mouth

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:39.678
and then blow it out
on the balcony

00:09:39.678 --> 00:09:43.182
and then come back, inhale
the smoke out of her mouth,

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:45.818
like a baby bird.

00:09:45.818 --> 00:09:48.687
And I'm like, "Do I want to
watch this with my daughter?"

00:09:48.687 --> 00:09:49.755
-Probably a good decision.

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:50.990
I think let her find it
on her own.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:52.625
Hopefully, one day,
she'll go off to college,

00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:53.993
and people will say,
"Oh, my God.

00:09:53.993 --> 00:09:55.261
Your dad was in
the funniest show."

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.962
-She's like, "No, he wasn't.

00:09:56.962 --> 00:09:58.163
That's not my dad."

00:09:58.163 --> 00:09:59.464
-Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:59.464 --> 00:10:00.499
